WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.137
Begin by avoiding an external process when
a suitable in-process library can provide

2
00:00:05.217 --> 00:00:07.406
the capability with a smaller boundary.

3
00:00:07.486 --> 00:00:11.544
When a child process is necessary, define
a narrow application operation.

4
00:00:11.624 --> 00:00:15.230
Select the executable from trusted
configuration or a closed map,

5
00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:19.625
prefer direct execution, and keep
shell-enabled behavior rare and explicitly

6
00:00:19.705 --> 00:00:20.285
reviewed.

7
00:00:20.365 --> 00:00:22.619
The application owns the argument array;

8
00:00:22.699 --> 00:00:27.013
clients supply only validated and
authorized values for designated operands.

9
00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:30.635
Direct execution and argument separation
remove shell parsing,

10
00:00:30.715 --> 00:00:32.776
but they do not authorize an argument.

11
00:00:32.856 --> 00:00:35.175
Understand the target program's grammar,

12
00:00:35.255 --> 00:00:37.509
map formats and modes to approved values,

13
00:00:37.589 --> 00:00:40.873
handle option-prefixed operands as the
utility documents,

14
00:00:40.953 --> 00:00:43.787
and never forward a client-supplied
argument list.

15
00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:48.117
TypeScript types help maintain the
internal model but do not validate runtime

16
00:00:48.197 --> 00:00:48.970
request data.

17
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.240
Executable control, input validation,

18
00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:55.699
and target-program option control are
related but separate responsibilities.

19
00:00:55.779 --> 00:00:58.355
Treat process context as part of the
operation.

20
00:00:58.435 --> 00:01:02.814
Map storage identifiers to controlled
paths, use private temporary locations,

21
00:01:02.894 --> 00:01:04.697
set a trusted working directory,

22
00:01:04.777 --> 00:01:08.319
and provide a minimal environment with
only required variables.

23
00:01:08.399 --> 00:01:10.396
Run with the least practical access.

24
00:01:10.476 --> 00:01:14.855
Bound input size, execution time,
concurrency, output, and temporary storage.

25
00:01:14.935 --> 00:01:17.318
Prefer asynchronous APIs in request paths,

26
00:01:17.398 --> 00:01:19.845
handle every exit and cancellation
outcome,

27
00:01:19.925 --> 00:01:24.047
and test cleanup and descendant-process
behavior on supported platforms.

28
00:01:24.127 --> 00:01:28.248
Finally, centralize launches behind small
purpose-specific interfaces,

29
00:01:28.328 --> 00:01:32.836
inventory every escape hatch, and test the
exact executable, arguments, options,

30
00:01:32.916 --> 00:01:33.947
and failure paths.

31
00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:36.667
Shell-injection prevention, least
privilege,

32
00:01:36.747 --> 00:01:40.482
and resource control solve different
problems and belong together.

33
00:01:40.562 --> 00:01:44.555
A secure process launch exposes a small
application-defined operation,

34
00:01:44.635 --> 00:01:46.954
not a general-purpose command interface.

35
00:01:47.034 --> 00:01:50.962
That principle remains useful across
Node.js APIs, operating systems,

36
00:01:51.042 --> 00:01:53.940
external tools, and future implementation
changes.
